  "image_name": "779390d11891895d251a77a8b3ced578.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified nutrient-flow diagram centered on U.S. corn and shows how the nitrogen in the crop is divided among food, feed, and fuel pathways. In the middle, corn is labeled as containing 4.3 Tg N, and three main arrows distribute that nitrogen: about 19% (0.8 Tg N) goes directly to food, 39% (1.7 Tg N) goes to feed, and 42% (1.8 Tg N) goes to fuel.\n\nThe diagram also highlights feedbacks between these sectors. A large curved arrow from fuel to feed indicates that ethanol production generates co-products returning nitrogen to animal feed, labeled 1.4 Tg N. Another curved arrow from feed back to food represents animal-derived food production. A smaller return arrow from feed toward the corn/food system is labeled manure recovery, suggesting recycled nutrients. On the left, fuel is linked to bioenergy production from food waste, and an additional orange arrow points from a nitrogen source symbol toward the corn/fuel system, implying external nitrogen inputs.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that corn is not partitioned into isolated end uses; instead, fuel, feed, and food are interconnected through nitrogen recycling and co-product flows.",
